---
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import { PromptBlock } from "../components/PromptBlock";
import { StepNav } from "../components/StepNav";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { LEADER_NOTES, LEARNING_OBJECTIVES, LINKS, PROMPTS, REPO_URL, STEPS } from "../lib/site";

const stepByIndex = (i: number) => STEPS[i];
---

<BaseLayout>
	<Hero slot="hero" />
	<StepNav slot="nav" client:load />

	<div class="leader-toolbar">
		<button type="button" id="leader-toggle" class="leader-toggle-btn" title="Presenter notes. Removed before launch.">
			🎤 Hide room lead notes
		</button>
		<span class="leader-toggle-hint">For the presenter · removed before launch</span>
	</div>

	<section class="intro-card">
		<h2 class="intro-title">What you'll learn</h2>
		<ul class="intro-objectives">
			{LEARNING_OBJECTIVES.map((objective) => <li>{objective}</li>)}
		</ul>
		<div class="intro-legend">
			<div class="legend-item">
				<span class="legend-badge legend-badge--why">Why</span>
				<span>The concept behind the step</span>
			</div>
			<div class="legend-item">
				<span class="legend-badge legend-badge--do">Do</span>
				<span>The action you (or your agent) takes</span>
			</div>
			<div class="legend-item">
				<span class="legend-badge legend-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<span>What success looks like</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 0: Prereqs ─── */}
	<article id="prereqs" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(0).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(0).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Gather these five things before you start. You probably have most of them already.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="checklist">
				<div class="checklist-item">
					<span class="checklist-icon" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
					<div>
						<p class="checklist-title">Node.js, git, and a terminal</p>
						<p class="checklist-note">Standard developer setup. You don't need Wrangler installed globally; your agent runs it with <code>npx</code> as it goes.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="checklist-item">
					<span class="checklist-icon" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
					<div>
						<p class="checklist-title">A coding agent</p>
						<p class="checklist-note">Whatever you already use. <a href="https://opencode.ai">OpenCode</a> works if you don't have one.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="checklist-item">
					<span class="checklist-icon" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
					<div>
						<p class="checklist-title">A GitHub account</p>
						<p class="checklist-note">The deploy button creates a repo in it.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="checklist-item">
					<span class="checklist-icon" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
					<div>
						<p class="checklist-title">A free Cloudflare account</p>
						<p class="checklist-note">No credit card required. <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up">Sign up</a> now, so it's one less thing to do mid-step.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="checklist-item">
					<span class="checklist-icon" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
					<div>
						<p class="checklist-title">Wrangler logged in to that account</p>
						<p class="checklist-note">Your agent runs Wrangler commands for you, and it can't finish a browser login on your behalf. Do it now: run <code>npx wrangler login</code>, then <code>npx wrangler whoami</code> to confirm the right account. If you have access to more than one Cloudflare account, set <code>CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID</code> to the one you're using (the ID is in the <code>whoami</code> output) so Wrangler doesn't stop to ask.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="tip">
				<span class="tip-label">Recommended</span>
				<p><a href="https://cli.github.com/">GitHub CLI</a> (<code>gh</code>), authenticated. Your agent opens the pull request for you in Step 3; without it, your agent hands you a link to open the PR yourself instead. Install it for your OS from the <a href="https://github.com/cli/cli#installation">GitHub CLI install guide</a>, then run <code>gh auth login</code>.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<article class="stepcard leader-card">
		<span class="leader-card-badge">🎤 For the room lead</span>
		<ul class="leader-card-list">
			{LEADER_NOTES.prereqs?.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Step 1: Deploy ─── */}
	<article id="deploy" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(1).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(1).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">One click. A repo in your account, a database, and a production Worker, wired up and ready to build your first Preview from.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--why">Why</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>You need a real, deployed app before you can create a Preview of it. Clicking the button below creates that app: the production Worker you'll build your first Preview from in the next step.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Do</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Clicking the button below starts a short guided flow on the Cloudflare dashboard:</p>
					<ol class="steps-inline">
						<li>Choose the GitHub account where the button should create your copy of the repo.</li>
						<li><strong>Keep the default names</strong> for the project and database. The prompts, URLs, and cleanup steps later in this guide use them.</li>
						<li>Create a <strong>new</strong> D1 database, not an existing one.</li>
						<li>Leave <strong>ENVIRONMENT</strong> set to <code>production</code>. It drives the badge at the top of the app.</li>
						<li><strong>Workers Builds</strong> (Cloudflare's own CI/CD) connects to that repo automatically. Nothing to configure.</li>
						<li>Before you finish, confirm <strong>Worker Previews</strong> is enabled.</li>
						<li>Leave the <strong>Build</strong>, <strong>Deploy</strong>, and <strong>Preview</strong> commands as the defaults it pre-fills. You don't need to touch them.</li>
					</ol>
					<p class="phase-note">No custom GitHub Actions, no secrets to paste.</p>

					<details class="aside">
						<summary>Show me each screen of the deploy flow</summary>
						<div class="aside-body">
							<Figure
								src="/screenshots/deploy-01-github.png"
								width={1400}
								height={647}
								alt="The Git account dropdown in the deploy form, listing connected GitHub and GitLab accounts and options to add a new connection."
								caption="1. Pick the Git account for your copy of the repo. Use a personal GitHub account; work organizations with SSO can block the Cloudflare GitHub app install."
							/>
							<Figure
								src="/screenshots/deploy-02-form.png"
								width={1400}
								height={639}
								alt="The deploy form with project name worker-previews-starter, Create new selected for the D1 database, and the database named activity-log-db."
								caption="2. Keep the default project name, choose Create new, and keep the database name activity-log-db."
							/>
							<Figure
								src="/screenshots/deploy-03-build-settings.png"
								width={1400}
								height={642}
								alt="The deploy form showing ENVIRONMENT set to production, an empty build command, deploy command npm run deploy, and preview command npx wrangler preview."
								caption="3. Leave ENVIRONMENT as production and the commands as they are."
							/>
							<Figure
								src="/screenshots/deploy-04-preview-settings.png"
								width={1400}
								height={646}
								alt="The bottom of the deploy form with Enable Preview builds switched on and Protect with Cloudflare Access switched off, next to the Deploy button."
								caption="4. Check that Enable Preview builds is on, then click Deploy."
							/>
							<Figure
								alt="The Worker dashboard after refreshing, with the Visit button in the top right."
								placeholderLabel="Placeholder: after the first build succeeds and you refresh, the Visit button in the top right."
								caption="5. When the build log says Success, refresh the page. Visit opens your production app."
							/>
						</div>
					</details>

					<div class="deploy-cta-wrap">
						<a
							href={`https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/?url=${REPO_URL}`}
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
							class="deploy-cta-link"
						>
							<img src="https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/button" alt="Deploy to Cloudflare" />
						</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>When the build log says <strong>Success</strong>, refresh the page. The dashboard doesn't update on its own after the first build, so the <strong>Visit</strong> button (top right) and the Domains toggles can look missing or off until you refresh. Then use <strong>Visit</strong> to open the production URL. (The <code>workers.dev</code> URL at the bottom of the build log works too.)</p>
					<p>You should see an <strong>Activity Log</strong> with a green <strong>Production</strong> badge and three seeded entries. Try adding an entry and deleting one, get a feel for the app before you branch it. Remember what's in there. You'll compare it against your Preview shortly.</p>
					<Figure
						src="/screenshots/app-production-seeded.png"
						width={960}
						height={716}
						size="narrow"
						alt="The production Activity Log with a green Production badge and three seeded entries: Wired up Workers Builds, Provisioned the D1 database, and Deployed the production Worker."
						caption="Your production app: three seeded rows. This is the data your Preview will not have."
					/>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="step-next">
				<a href="#isolate" class="step-next-link">
					<span class="step-next-hint">Next</span>
					Configure Previews
					<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<article class="stepcard leader-card">
		<span class="leader-card-badge">🎤 For the room lead</span>
		<ul class="leader-card-list">
			{LEADER_NOTES.deploy?.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Step 2: Isolate ─── */}
	<article id="isolate" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(2).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(2).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Give every Preview a production-safe D1 binding and its own observability settings.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Setup</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Ask your agent to clone the repo the button just created and start a working branch:</p>
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Copy this prompt into your agent" text={PROMPTS.cloneAndBranch} />
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--why">Why</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>
						Previews inherit your <strong>code</strong> automatically. Their variables, secrets, bindings, and observability come from separate Preview configuration, so production resources are never used accidentally.
					</p>

					<div class="concept-diagram">
						<div class="concept-col">
							<span class="concept-col-head concept-col-head--prod">Production</span>
							<div class="concept-cell">code</div>
							<div class="concept-cell concept-cell--bind">DB → prod-db</div>
						</div>
						<div class="concept-col">
							<span class="concept-col-head concept-col-head--preview">Preview</span>
							<div class="concept-cell concept-cell--inherit">code (inherited)</div>
							<div class="concept-cell concept-cell--empty">DB → ??? (empty!)</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<p>D1, KV, R2, and most other resources bind by ID. You will create one D1 database that is isolated from production and retain it as this Worker's shared Preview database.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Do</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Your repo already has a <code>previews</code> block. It sets one variable, <code>ENVIRONMENT = "preview"</code>, which is why the app's badge will say <strong>Preview</strong> instead of <strong>Production</strong>. Your agent adds a D1 database to that block. This is the shape of the change, shown so you know what to expect. You don't need to edit it by hand:</p>
					<div class="cmd cmd--json">
						<div class="cmd-bar"><span class="cmd-label">wrangler.json (reference only)</span></div>
						<pre><code>{`{
  "vars": { "ENVIRONMENT": "production" },
  "d1_databases": [
    { "binding": "DB", "database_name": "activity-log-db" } // production
  ],
  "previews": {
    "vars": { "ENVIRONMENT": "preview" },        // already there
    "d1_databases": [                             // new
      { "binding": "DB", "database_name": "workshop-preview-db" }
    ],
    "observability": { "enabled": true }          // new
  }
}`}</code></pre>
					</div>
					<p class="phase-note">Same binding name (<code>DB</code>), different database. This block will remain on <code>main</code> after the PR merges. See <a href={LINKS.configDocs}>Previews configuration</a> for everything the block can hold.</p>
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Copy this prompt into your agent" text={PROMPTS.isolateResource} />
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p><code>wrangler.json</code> now points Previews at <code>workshop-preview-db</code>, enables Preview observability, leaves production untouched, and has the change committed.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<details class="aside">
				<summary>Why doesn't this apply to Durable Objects?</summary>
				<div class="aside-body">
					<p>
						Durable Objects and Containers are the actual exception. When the class lives in the same Worker, each Preview gets its own namespace and storage automatically (no override needed) because that state belongs to the Worker itself, not to an external resource identified by an ID. D1, KV, and R2 are the norm, not the exception: bind by ID, and anything sharing that ID shares the data.
					</p>
				</div>
			</details>

			<div class="step-next">
				<a href="#preview" class="step-next-link">
					<span class="step-next-hint">Next</span>
					Open your Preview
					<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<article class="stepcard leader-card">
		<span class="leader-card-badge">🎤 For the room lead</span>
		<ul class="leader-card-list">
			{LEADER_NOTES.isolate?.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Step 3: Preview ─── */}
	<article id="preview" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(3).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(3).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Push the branch, open a pull request, and let Workers Builds run <code>wrangler preview</code>.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Do</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Ask your agent to push and open the PR:</p>
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Copy this prompt into your agent" text={PROMPTS.openPullRequest} />
					<p class="phase-note">Then check it yourself: open GitHub, find the repository the Deploy to Cloudflare button created in your account, and open the pull request your agent just made.</p>
					<p class="phase-note">Within a minute or two, <strong>Workers Builds</strong> deploys the Preview and comments the URL on your pull request. You can also open the <strong>Workers Builds</strong> check on the PR, or go straight to <code>https://&lt;branch&gt;-&lt;worker-name&gt;.&lt;your-subdomain&gt;.workers.dev</code> (for this workshop: <code>isolate-preview-db-worker-previews-starter.&lt;your-subdomain&gt;.workers.dev</code>).</p>
					<Figure
						src="/screenshots/pr-bot-comment.png"
						width={1400}
						height={910}
						alt="The cloudflare-workers-and-pages bot comment on the pull request, showing the Preview URL and a table of Preview deployments by commit with build and deployment status."
						caption="Where the Preview URL shows up. The Workers Builds check on the PR and the direct workers.dev URL both work too."
					/>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Open the Preview URL from the PR. The badge now says <strong>Preview</strong>, and the page explains that the D1 database is connected but has no schema yet.</p>
					<Figure
						src="/screenshots/app-preview-needs-schema.png"
						width={960}
						height={360}
						alt="The Preview Activity Log with an orange Preview badge, explaining that its database is connected but doesn't have the workshop schema yet."
						caption="A missing schema is an expected setup state, not a broken page."
					/>
					<div class="tip tip--pin">
						<span class="tip-label">Keep both tabs open</span>
						<p>Preview tab + production tab from Step 1. You'll flip between them in the next step.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="step-next">
				<a href="#interact" class="step-next-link">
					<span class="step-next-hint">Next</span>
					Apply the schema
					<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<article class="stepcard leader-card">
		<span class="leader-card-badge">🎤 For the room lead</span>
		<ul class="leader-card-list">
			{LEADER_NOTES.preview?.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Step 4: Prove ─── */}
	<article id="interact" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(4).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(4).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Apply a Preview-only candidate schema, then test the real user workflow.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--why">Why</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Creating a D1 database doesn't seed it. The Preview you opened last step is connected to a real database that has no tables and no rows yet, nothing to test against.</p>
					<p>What you're about to apply is a brand-new schema, one production doesn't have and never will. Testing a schema change directly in Preview, isolated from production, is exactly what this safety net is for.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Do</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>The SQL fixture lives outside <code>migrations/</code>, so production automation cannot discover it. Ask your agent to apply it to the Preview database by name:</p>
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Copy this prompt into your agent" text={PROMPTS.applyPreviewSchema} />
					<p class="phase-note">Using the account-level database name rather than the <code>DB</code> binding is deliberate: the binding in top-level Wrangler configuration is production. <code>--yes</code> skips Wrangler's "this may take some time" confirmation, which otherwise blocks a non-interactive agent. This runs against the live remote database, so the change is immediate; no redeploy needed before you test.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Refresh the Preview. It now shows three rows containing the word <strong>Preview</strong>. Add an entry and refresh: it persists. Then click <strong>Delete</strong>.</p>
					<Figure
						src="/screenshots/app-preview-delete-error.png"
						width={960}
						height={828}
						size="narrow"
						alt="The Preview Activity Log with three Preview seed rows and a red error: Delete failed. Your data is unchanged. Check this Preview's Observability logs."
						caption="The candidate schema exposed a real compatibility bug without changing production."
					/>
					<details class="aside aside--spoiler">
						<summary>Did something break?</summary>
						<div class="aside-body">
							<p>Delete fails. The page shows an error and the row stays put. That's a real, deliberate compatibility bug the new schema exposed, not workshop breakage. Good thing this is a Preview: production never saw it. That's exactly what Previews are for, let's debug it in the next two steps.</p>
						</div>
					</details>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="step-next">
				<a href="#observability" class="step-next-link">
					<span class="step-next-hint">Next</span>
					Observability
					<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<article class="stepcard leader-card">
		<span class="leader-card-badge">🎤 For the room lead</span>
		<ul class="leader-card-list">
			{LEADER_NOTES.interact?.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Step 5: Observability ─── */}
	<article id="observability" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(5).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(5).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Use the failed request's structured log to find the schema mismatch.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Do</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>You just saw the error on the page. Let's investigate it properly: open the Cloudflare dashboard and watch this Preview's own live logs.</p>
					<ol class="steps-inline">
						<li>Go to <strong>Workers &amp; Pages</strong> → your Worker.</li>
						<li>In the Worker header, open the environment dropdown (it currently says <strong>Production</strong>) and select your branch name (for this workshop, <code>isolate-preview-db</code>).</li>
						<li>Open the <strong>Observability</strong> tab and switch on <strong>Live</strong> so you can watch requests arrive in real time.</li>
					</ol>
					<Figure
						src="/screenshots/dash-env-dropdown.png"
						width={1400}
						height={456}
						alt="The Cloudflare dashboard Worker header with the environment dropdown open, showing Production and the isolate-preview-db branch."
						caption="The screen most people can't find on their own: switch the Worker from Production to your branch, then open Observability. Production's log list will not show the delete errors."
					/>
					<ol class="steps-inline" start="4">
						<li>Try Delete once more and watch the failed <code>DELETE</code> request land live, then open it in the log list.</li>
					</ol>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Find <code>activity_log.delete_failed</code>. Its D1 error names the missing column, that's the diagnosis: the query still references production's old column name. Hold onto that error text, you'll hand it to your agent in the next step.</p>
					<Figure
						src="/screenshots/dash-observability-delete-failed.png"
						width={1400}
						height={540}
						alt="The Observability tab with isolate-preview-db selected in the Worker header, listing error-level events for DELETE requests to the Preview URL."
						caption="Your branch's own live events: the failed DELETE requests show up here, not in Production. Click one to open activity_log.delete_failed and its D1 error."
					/>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="step-next">
				<a href="#final" class="step-next-link">
					<span class="step-next-hint">Next</span>
					Fix and merge
					<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<article class="stepcard leader-card">
		<span class="leader-card-badge">🎤 For the room lead</span>
		<ul class="leader-card-list">
			{LEADER_NOTES.observability?.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Step 6: Fix and merge ─── */}
	<article id="final" class="stepcard">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">{stepByIndex(6).num}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">{stepByIndex(6).label}</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Repair the incompatibility using the error you found, confirm the fix, then merge only the safe change.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Do</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Copy the error text from Observability, then give it to your agent together with this prompt:</p>
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Give the error and this prompt to your agent" text={PROMPTS.diagnoseAndFix} />
					<p class="phase-note">A change from <code>id</code> to <code>activity_id</code> would only fix the Preview. The repair must also work with production's unchanged schema.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--verify">Verify</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<p>Wait for Workers Builds to redeploy the same Preview URL. Your agent should have already confirmed add, refresh, and delete pass as part of the fix. Production should still show its original, untouched rows.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="phase">
				<span class="phase-badge phase-badge--do">Merge</span>
				<div class="phase-content">
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Copy this prompt into your agent" text={PROMPTS.mergePullRequest} />
					<p class="phase-note"><code>workshop-preview-db</code> doesn't disappear when you merge. It becomes this Worker's shared Preview database for every future branch. Want an isolated database per branch instead? Apply migrations and seed data separately for each one, rather than pointing every Preview at the same database.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Recap ─── */}
	<article id="recap" class="stepcard stepcard--wrapup">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge stepcard-badge--wrapup">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">✓</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">What you just did</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">The same loop, start to finish: build, isolate, break, diagnose, fix, merge.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<p class="wrapup-p">You deployed a real production Worker, then opened a branch and gave it its own Preview: isolated code, an isolated D1 database, and its own Observability. You applied a brand-new schema straight to that Preview and never to production, found a genuine compatibility bug by using the app yourself, diagnosed it from that Preview's own live logs, and fixed it, all without production ever being at risk.</p>
			<p class="wrapup-p">That's what Worker Previews are for: every change, however small or risky, gets its own live environment with its own URL, settings, and logs, where it can prove itself before it gets anywhere near production. Merging promoted only the code you verified. The Preview-only schema never reached production, and <code>workshop-preview-db</code> stays behind as the shared database for this Worker's future Previews.</p>
			<p class="wrapup-p">Keep going: read the <a href={LINKS.blog}>Worker Previews announcement</a>, or dig into the <a href={LINKS.docs}>Previews docs</a> for custom domains, secrets, and per-branch resources.</p>
		</div>
	</article>

	{/* ─── Wrap-up ─── */}
	<article id="wrap-up" class="stepcard stepcard--wrapup">
		<div class="stepcard-head">
			<div class="stepcard-badge stepcard-badge--wrapup">
				<span class="stepcard-badge-num">✓</span>
			</div>
			<div class="stepcard-head-copy">
				<h2 class="stepcard-title">Clean up</h2>
				<p class="stepcard-lede">Know what persists after the pull request closes.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stepcard-body">
			<p class="wrapup-p">The branch Preview can be removed after merge, but keep <code>workshop-preview-db</code>. Its binding is now part of <code>main</code> and it serves as the shared, production-safe database for future Previews. Those Previews are isolated from production but share this database with each other.</p>

			<details class="aside">
				<summary>Want everything gone?</summary>
				<div class="aside-body">
					<p>Optional. Closing the pull request only deletes the Preview deployment. The GitHub repo, Worker, and both D1 databases stay until you delete them.</p>
					<p>Default names (use yours if you changed them in the deploy form):</p>
					<ul class="tight-list">
						<li>GitHub repo: <code>&lt;you&gt;/worker-previews-starter</code></li>
						<li>Worker: <code>worker-previews-starter</code></li>
						<li>Production D1: <code>activity-log-db</code></li>
						<li>Preview D1: <code>workshop-preview-db</code></li>
					</ul>
					<PromptBlock client:load label="Copy this prompt into your agent" text={PROMPTS.cleanup} />
					<div class="cmd">
						<div class="cmd-bar"><span class="cmd-label">Terminal</span></div>
						<pre><code>npx wrangler delete worker-previews-starter
npx wrangler d1 delete activity-log-db
npx wrangler d1 delete workshop-preview-db
gh repo delete &lt;you&gt;/worker-previews-starter --yes</code></pre>
					</div>
					<p>Confirm each <code>wrangler</code> prompt. From the dashboard: Workers &amp; Pages → your Worker → Manage → Delete; D1 → each database → Delete; GitHub → Settings → Delete repository.</p>
				</div>
			</details>
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
	const leaderToggle = document.getElementById("leader-toggle");
	leaderToggle?.addEventListener("click", () => {
		document.body.classList.toggle("leader-notes-hidden");
		const hidden = document.body.classList.contains("leader-notes-hidden");
		leaderToggle.textContent = hidden ? "🎤 Show room lead notes" : "🎤 Hide room lead notes";
	});
</script>
